Template-Type: ReDIF-Article 1.0
Author-Name: Kasım Takım
Author-Email: kasimtakim@harran.edu.tr
Author-Workplace-Name: Harran Üniversitesi, Fen-Edebiyat Fakültesi, Kimya Bölümü
Title: Makineler Düşünüyor mu? Yapay Zekâda Suret İşleme ile Mana İdraki Ayrımı Üzerine Bir İnceleme
Abstract: Reports that large language models passed the Turing test with a high success rate in 2025 have revived the question of whether machines think. This study addresses that question not through behavioural criteria but through the concept of the apprehension of meaning, taken here as the constitutive element of thought. The article first demonstrates — relying on Turing's own designation of the procedure as an imitation game and on the caveats issued by the researchers who conducted the experiment — that the test measures behaviour rather than cognition. It then shows that the founding principle of the modern computational paradigm, traceable to Leibniz, is the processing of symbols independently of propositional content; accordingly, the claim that machines do not grasp meaning is not a defect attributed after the fact but a constitutive condition of the architecture itself. This thesis is supported by Searle's Chinese Room argument, by the phenomenon of hallucination in language models, and by experimental findings showing that reasoning models collapse beyond a complexity threshold. In its second axis, the study evaluates the matter within the classical Islamic taxonomy of substance, arguing that attributing agency to a device lacking the immaterial substance in which apprehension inheres constitutes both a confusion of concomitance with causation and an instance of anthropomorphism. The final section proposes that dataism, which reduces the human being to an algorithm, is the last link in this reductionist chain, and that the two opposing attitudes that treat the instrument as an agent — technological submission and technological apocalypticism — share a common category error. The article concludes that the principal concern is not that machines have begun to think, but that human beings have begun to delegate thinking to machines.
Keywords: Artificial intelligence, Turing test, apprehension of meaning, Chinese Room, dataism, rational soul, reductionism
